{%- comment -%}
  Shop-wide reviews — the real ones.

  Usage:
    {% render 'shop-reviews', show: 'summary' %}   average + count
    {% render 'shop-reviews', show: 'carousel' %}  the review cards

  ── What this replaces ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Six customer testimonials written into assets/app.js — names, emirates,
  the products they had supposedly bought, five stars each and a "Verified
  Purchase" tick — rendered on ten surfaces including every collection page.
  Alongside them, "4.8, based on 10,284 verified reviews" typed into two
  editorial sections.

  None of it described anyone. It came from the static build the theme was
  ported from, where it was placeholder copy; products and brands were both
  switched to live data during that port and the testimonials were missed.

  Invented reviews are the one piece of social proof that is worth less than
  nothing: a store that fabricates them is making an unsubstantiated claim
  about real named people, and a shopper who recognises one stops believing
  the authenticity guarantee too — which is this store's whole proposition.

  ── What it does instead ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Reads Judge.me, which already holds the store's real reviews and already
  renders them on the product page. Nothing is rendered unless Judge.me
  reports at least one shop review, and the caller is told so through
  data-reviews-empty, so a heading never sits above an empty space.

  A store with no shop reviews yet therefore shows no reviews section. That
  is the correct state: it is also the thing to fix, by turning on Judge.me's
  shop-review requests — see docs/judgeme-review-requests.md.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  # all_reviews_*, not shop_reviews_*. Judge.me publishes both: shop_reviews
  # counts only reviews about the shop itself (3), all_reviews counts those
  # plus every product review (24). The carousel below shows all of them, so
  # the summary has to count the same set or the two contradict each other on
  # the same screen.
  assign sr_count = shop.metafields.judgeme.all_reviews_count | plus: 0
  assign sr_rating = shop.metafields.judgeme.all_reviews_rating
  assign sr_mode = show | default: 'carousel'
-%}

{%- if sr_count > 0 -%}
  {%- if sr_mode == 'summary' -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Stars only when there is an average behind them. A count on its own is
      still true; five stars on nothing is the thing this file exists to
      remove.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="rv-summary fade-up">
      {%- if sr_rating != blank -%}
        <div class="rv-big">{{ sr_rating | times: 1.0 | round: 1 }}</div>
      {%- endif -%}
      <div class="rv-details">
        {%- if sr_rating != blank -%}
          {% render 'star-rating', rating: sr_rating %}
        {%- endif -%}
        <p>Based on {{ sr_count }} verified review{% unless sr_count == 1 %}s{% endunless %}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  {%- else -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The metafield, not an empty container.

      The first version of this rendered <div class="jdgm-carousel"></div> and
      trusted Judge.me's script to find and fill it. It never did: the heading
      shipped over an empty box, which is the exact failure this snippet was
      written to prevent, just with the fabricated reviews taken out.

      judgeme.featured_carousel holds the widget already rendered — six real
      reviews, about 4 KB of HTML, written by the app whenever reviews change.
      Server-rendered means it is there at first paint, needs no round trip,
      and cannot shift the layout when a script arrives late.

      The wrapper div is Judge.me's own documented requirement; the widget
      sizes its slides against it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Built from judgeme.reviews_grid, not judgeme.featured_carousel.

      Both are written by the app. featured_carousel is pre-rendered HTML of
      whatever is marked Featured in Judge.me, which here is three reviews —
      so the carousel showed three out of forty-eight however wide the screen.
      reviews_grid is the same data as JSON, 25 of them, which is enough to
      scroll through and lets the count be a setting rather than a trip into
      another app's admin.

      Judge.me's own class names on purpose. Every rule the theme already has
      for this widget keeps applying, and the arrows below bind to the same
      markup, so nothing here depends on which of the two sources rendered it.

      Falls back to the app's HTML if the JSON is ever missing.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign sr_grid = shop.metafields.judgeme.reviews_grid.value -%}
    {%- assign sr_pool = sr_grid.all_reviews.reviews -%}

    <div style="clear:both"></div>
    <div class="jdgm-carousel-wrapper">
      {%- if sr_pool and sr_pool.size > 0 -%}
        <div class="jdgm-widget jdgm-carousel jdgm-carousel--default-theme">
          <div class="jdgm-carousel__item-container">
            <div class="jdgm-carousel__item-wrapper">
              {%- assign sr_min = settings.reviews_min_rating | default: 4 -%}
              {%- assign sr_max = settings.reviews_max_cards | default: 12 -%}
              {%- assign sr_shown = 0 -%}
              {%- for sr in sr_pool -%}
                {%- if sr_shown >= sr_max -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}
                {%- if sr.rating < sr_min -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
                {%- if sr.body == blank -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
                {%- assign sr_shown = sr_shown | plus: 1 -%}
                {%- liquid
                  assign sr_who = sr.reviewer_name | default: 'Anonymous'
                  if sr.is_anonymous_reviewer
                    assign sr_who = 'Anonymous'
                  endif
                -%}
                <div class="jdgm-carousel-item">
                  <div class="jdgm-carousel-item__review">
                    <div class="jdgm-carousel-item__review-rating" role="img" aria-label="{{ sr.rating }} out of 5 stars">
                      {%- for i in (1..5) -%}
                        <span class="jdgm-star {% if i <= sr.rating %}jdgm--on{% else %}jdgm--off{% endif %}"></span>
                      {%- endfor -%}
                    </div>
                    <div class="jdgm-carousel-item__review-content">
                      {%- if sr.title != blank -%}<div class="jdgm-carousel-item__review-title">{{ sr.title }}</div>{%- endif -%}
                      <div class="jdgm-carousel-item__review-body">{{ sr.body }}</div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="jdgm-carousel-item__reviewer-name-wrapper">
                    <div class="jdgm-carousel-item__reviewer-name jdgm-ellipsis">{{ sr_who }}</div>
                    <div class="jdgm-carousel-item__timestamp jdgm-ellipsis">{{ sr.created_at | date: '%-d %b %Y' }}</div>
                  </div>
                  {%- comment -%}
                    Linked, unlike the app's version. A review that names a
                    bottle and does not let you reach it is a dead end on a
                    page whose whole job is to send people to products.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  {%- if sr.product_title != blank and sr.product_in_store and sr.product_url != blank -%}
                    <a class="jdgm-carousel-item__product" href="{{ sr.product_url }}" title="{{ sr.product_title | escape }}">
                      {%- if sr.product_pictures_url != blank -%}
                        <img class="jdgm-carousel-item__product-image" src="{{ sr.product_pictures_url }}" alt="{{ sr.product_title | escape }}" loading="lazy" width="54" height="54">
                      {%- endif -%}
                    </a>
                  {%- endif -%}
                </div>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="jdgm-carousel__arrows">
            <div class="jdgm-carousel__left-arrow" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Previous reviews"></div>
            <div class="jdgm-carousel__right-arrow" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Next reviews"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      {%- else -%}
        {{ shop.metafields.judgeme.featured_carousel }}
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      The arrows Judge.me renders are inert.

      Their legacy carousel script is what binds them, and it is not on the
      page: the app embed loads the loader for their newer snippet widget,
      while this metafield renders the older carousel. So the markup arrives
      with two buttons that look like controls and do nothing — which is
      worse than having none, because a shopper reads three reviews and
      assumes there are no more.

      The track is a scroll container (theme.css lays it out), so one card's
      width of scrollLeft is the whole behaviour. No slide maths, no state,
      nothing that drifts out of step with what is on screen.

      Guarded against running twice, and against the day Judge.me's own
      script does load and binds them itself.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <script>
      (function () {
        function bind(root) {
          if (!root || root.dataset.tfsArrows) return;
          var track = root.querySelector('.jdgm-carousel__item-wrapper');
          var prev  = root.querySelector('.jdgm-carousel__left-arrow');
          var next  = root.querySelector('.jdgm-carousel__right-arrow');
          if (!track || !prev || !next) return;
          root.dataset.tfsArrows = '1';

          function step(dir) {
            var card = track.querySelector('.jdgm-carousel-item');
            var by = card ? card.getBoundingClientRect().width : track.clientWidth;
            track.scrollBy({ left: dir * by, behavior: 'smooth' });
          }
          function paint() {
            /* Nothing to scroll to is not a disabled button, it is a button
               that should not be there: with three reviews on a wide screen
               the whole set is already visible. */
            var over = track.scrollWidth - track.clientWidth > 2;
            root.querySelector('.jdgm-carousel__arrows').style.display = over ? '' : 'none';
          }
          prev.addEventListener('click', function (e) { e.preventDefault(); step(-1); });
          next.addEventListener('click', function (e) { e.preventDefault(); step(1); });
          /* They are divs with role=button, so Enter and Space are ours to handle. */
          [prev, next].forEach(function (b) {
            b.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) {
              if (e.key !== 'Enter' && e.key !== ' ') return;
              e.preventDefault(); step(b === prev ? -1 : 1);
            });
          });
          paint();
          addEventListener('resize', paint);
        }
        function init() { document.querySelectorAll('.jdgm-carousel-wrapper .jdgm-carousel').forEach(bind); }
        if (document.readyState === 'loading') document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', init);
        else init();
      })();
    </script>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Nothing real to show. The marker lets the caller drop its own heading
    rather than leaving "What Customers Say" above a gap.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <span hidden data-reviews-empty></span>
{%- endif -%}
